      },
      "text": "David Baltimore (1971, Nobel 1975) proposed classifying viruses by the relationship\nbetween their genome and mRNA production. This is more fundamental than host range\nor disease type because it reveals the replication strategy."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-BALTIMORE-CLASSIFICATION.md#003",
      "path": [
        "02-BALTIMORE-CLASSIFICATION.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:virology:02-baltimore-classification, git-history:virology:02-baltimore-classification]",
        "canonical_path": "virology/02-BALTIMORE-CLASSIFICATION.md",
        "concepts": "[baltimore, classification]",
        "current_path": "virology/02-BALTIMORE-CLASSIFICATION.md",
        "id": "maxim:virology:baltimore-classification",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "virology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[baltimore, classification]",
        "section": "virology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Baltimore Classification System",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│              BALTIMORE CLASSIFICATION — ALL 7 CLASSES            │\n│                                                                  │\n│  The Central Question:                                           │\n│  \"How does the virus produce mRNA from its genome?\"              │\n│                                                                  │\n│  Class I   dsDNA    → mRNA  (like host; use host or viral pol)   │\n│  Class II  ssDNA    → dsDNA → mRNA                               │\n│  Class III dsRNA    → mRNA  (viral RdRp required)                │\n│  Class IV  +ssRNA   = mRNA  (genome IS the mRNA)                 │\n│  Class V   -ssRNA   → mRNA  (viral RdRp required for first step) │\n│  Class VI  ssRNA-RT → DNA   → mRNA  (reverse transcription)      │\n│  Class VII dsDNA-RT → mRNA  (with RT in replication cycle)       │\n│                                                                  │\n│  KEY DIVISION: RNA vs DNA genome                                 │\n│  DNA viruses: generally lower mutation rate, larger genomes      │\n│  RNA viruses: very high mutation rate, smaller genomes           │\n│  Retroviruses: RNA genome → DNA intermediate → integrates        │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```"
    },
    {
